</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>For a simple random walk with step probability <m>p</m>, compute</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X_i].</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>For the same walk, compute</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Var}(X_i).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[S_n]</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Var}(S_n).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>For a symmetric random walk, find</p>

<p>
<md>P(S_4=0).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Let</p>

<p>
<md>P
=
\begin{pmatrix}
0.7&amp;0.3\\
0.2&amp;0.8
\end{pmatrix}.</md>
</p>

<p>Verify that <m>P</m> is a transition matrix.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Compute</p>

<p>
<md>P^2</md>
</p>

<p>for the previous matrix.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the stationary distribution of the previous chain.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>N(t)</md>
</p>

<p>is a Poisson process with rate <m>5</m> per hour.</p>

<p>Find</p>

<p>
<md>P(N(2)=7).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>For the same process, find the distribution of</p>

<p>
<md>N(5)-N(3).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>For the same process, find the expected waiting time until the next
event.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the distribution of the time until the fourth event.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Suppose a branching process has offspring PGF</p>

<p>
<md>G(s)=0.4+0.6s^2.</md>
</p>

<p>Write the extinction fixed-point equation.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Prove that</p>

<p>
<md>S_n=2H_n-n</md>
</p>

<p>for a simple random walk, where <m>H_n</m> is the number of <m>+1</m> steps.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the distribution of <m>S_n</m> from the binomial distribution of
<m>H_n</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Prove the Chapman--Kolmogorov equations for a discrete Markov chain.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show that if</p>

<p>
<md>\boldsymbol{\pi}P=\boldsymbol{\pi},</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boldsymbol{\pi}P^n=\boldsymbol{\pi}</md>
</p>

<p>for every positive integer <m>n</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show that detailed balance implies stationarity.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Classify the states of a finite Markov chain into communicating classes
for a transition matrix of your choice.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive</p>

<p>
<md>N(t)
\sim
\operatorname{Poisson}(\lambda t)</md>
</p>

<p>from the Poisson-process increment definition.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show that the first interarrival time of a Poisson process is
exponential.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show that the <m>n</m>-th Poisson arrival time has a gamma distribution.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Prove that the superposition of two independent Poisson processes is
Poisson.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Prove the thinning property for a Poisson process.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[Z_n]=m^n</md>
</p>

<p>for a Galton--Watson process with <m>Z_0=1</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>For a branching process, explain why the extinction probability
satisfies</p>

<p>
<md>q=G(q).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show that a centered random walk is a martingale.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show that</p>

<p>
<md>S_n^2-n\sigma^2</md>
</p>

<p>is a martingale for independent mean-zero increments with variance
<m>\sigma^2</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show that the first hitting time of a state is a stopping time.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Use martingale ideas to derive the symmetric gambler's ruin probability</p>

<p>
<md>P_i(\text{hit }N\text{ before }0)
=
\frac{i}{N}.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the Brownian covariance formula</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Cov}(B_s,B_t)
=
\min(s,t).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
